{
  "sticker": "owl",
  "skeleton": "bird standing facing the viewer: root at the feet, body with a belly bone for squash, head on the body, a face bone carrying eyes, brows and beak; a folded/open right wing, the far (left) wing shown only open in flight; two feet on the root.",
  "sheets": [
    {
      "id": "parts",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "prompt": "Image 1 is a sticker of an owl character. Draw this exact owl as a cut-out paper puppet: its separate body parts laid out on one sheet, the same drawing style, colours, feather texture, line and proportions as in image 1, every part at the size it has in image 1.\n\nDraw each part on its own, spread out over the sheet with wide empty gaps between them, no part touching or overlapping another:\n1. the body: the round chubby body with its cream speckled belly and brown sides, facing the viewer as in image 1, with no head, no wings and no feet. Its top is a plain rounded dome (it will be hidden under the head).\n2. the head: the round head with its ear tufts and its pale facial disc, facing the viewer as in image 1, with a completely blank face — no eyes, no eyelids, no beak, no brows; keep the rosy cheeks. Its bottom edge is a full, smooth, rounded edge.\n3. the folded wing: the owl's right wing folded at rest, as it lies against the body on the right of image 1, drawn whole, including the top part normally hidden under the head, with a rounded shoulder at the top.\n4. the open wing: the same wing fully spread out as in flight, long flight feathers fanned out, the wide rounded shoulder at its base, seen from the front.\n5. one foot: a yellow foot with three toes spread, as in image 1, with a short feathered leg stub on top.\n\nNo text, no labels, no numbers, no outline around the parts beyond the drawing's own line, no shadows. Fully transparent background."
    },
    {
      "id": "face",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "references": [
        "parts"
      ],
      "prompt": "Image 1 is a sticker of an owl character; image 2 shows the same owl cut into puppet parts, with its head drawn with a blank face. Draw the owl's face pieces as separate stickers on one sheet: the same drawing style, colours and line as in image 1, every piece at the size it has on the owl in image 1, so it fits the blank head in image 2.\n\nDraw each piece on its own, spread out in two tidy rows with wide empty gaps between them, nothing touching:\n1. the owl's left eye (on the right of image 1) as it is in image 1: calm and sleepy, a dark brown eye with a white sparkle under a heavy, half-closed tan eyelid, with its thin dark lashes and the small curved brow above it.\n2. the same eye wide open and alert: a big round dark eye with a white sparkle, no eyelid over it, the same brow raised a little.\n3. the same eye gently closed in sleep: a soft downward-curving line with a few lashes on a tan eyelid, the same brow.\n4. the same eye closed in a happy smile: an upward-curving arc, the same brow.\n5. the same eye sad: a big round dark eye looking down, its brow tilted up in the middle.\n6. the beak, closed, as in image 1: small, yellow-orange, pointing down.\n7. the beak wide open in a big yawn: the upper and lower beak wide apart, the inside of the mouth pink.\n8. the beak a little open in a soft round 'hoo'.\n\nNo text, no labels, no numbers, no shadows, no face around the pieces. Fully transparent background."
    }
  ],
